      "text": "First, we rewrite the given equation in the standard form of a circle using the completing the square method.\n\nThe given equation is:\n\\[ x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0 \\]\n\nWe rearrange the terms to group the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms together:\n\\[ (x^2 - 6x) + (y^2 + 2y) = -6 \\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for the \\(x\\) terms:\n\\[ x^2 - 6x \\]\nTo complete the square, we add and subtract \\(\\left(\\frac{-6}{2}\\right)^2 = 9\\):\n\\[ x^2 - 6x + 9 - 9 = (x - 3)^2 - 9 \\]\n\nNow, we complete the square for the \\(y\\) terms:\n\\[ y^2 + 2y \\]\nTo complete the square, we add and subtract \\(\\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right)^2 = 1\\):\n\\[ y^2 + 2y + 1 - 1 = (y + 1)^2 - 1 \\]\n\nSubstituting these back into the equation, we get:\n\\[ (x - 3)^2 - 9 + (y + 1)^2 - 1 = -6 \\]\n\nSimplifying the constants on the left side:\n\\[ (x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 - 10 = -6 \\]\n\\[ (x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4 \\]\n\nThe equation \\((x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4\\) is now in the standard form of a circle \\((x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\\), where \\((h, k)\\) is the center and \\(r\\) is the radius.\n\nFrom the equation \\((x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4\\), we can see that the center of the circle is \\((3, -1)\\) and the radius \\(r\\) is \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
