      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The given equation of the circle is \\(x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0\\). We need to rewrite this equation in the standard form \\((x-h)^2 + (y-k)^2 = r^2\\) by completing the square.\n\nFirst, consider the \\(x\\)-terms and the \\(y\\)-terms separately.\n\nFor the \\(x\\)-terms, \\(x^2 - 6x\\), we complete the square:\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x = (x-3)^2 - 9.\n\\]\n\nFor the \\(y\\)-terms, \\(y^2 + 2y\\), we complete the square:\n\\[\ny^2 + 2y = (y+1)^2 - 1.\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original equation:\n\\[\n(x-3)^2 - 9 + (y+1)^2 - 1 + 6 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\\[\n(x-3)^2 + (y+1)^2 - 4 = 0 \\implies (x-3)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 4.\n\\]\n\nThe equation \\((x-3)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 4\\) is now in the standard form of a circle's equation \\((x-h)^2 + (y-k)^2 = r^2\\), where \\((h, k)\\) is the center of the circle and \\(r\\) is the radius. From the equation, we can see that the center \\((h, k)\\) is \\((3, -1)\\) and the radius \\(r\\) is \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
